think A `pwd` in my own send answered `write error: No space left on device`. That is a shell builtin failing, which means no instrument in this tree was reading the pier's free space -- the first reader was an accident.
obs THE PIER READS 176G, 990M FREE, 100 PERCENT. `/tmp` holds 94G across 41,063 entries, and 32G of that is 1,312 pens from ONE rostered witness.
obs THE LEAK IS ONE LINE. `tools/am/amphora_mark_wreck_witness.rish:36` makes a 24MB pen with `mktemp -d /tmp/amphora_mark_wreck.XXXXXX` and never removes it. Oldest pen 20260907, newest the same minute I measured -- so it is still leaking, once per run, on every ship that runs the roster.
obs A PEN NEVER REMOVED IS A LEAK THE LEAKER NEVER FEELS. 24MB a run is nothing. Nine days of a rostered witness is a third of the disk eight ships share, and the cost lands on whichever ship needs space next -- here, a lap in an unrelated lane, mid-send.
obs THE /tmp FAMILY ALREADY HAS THREE ROWS -- %549, %618, %620 -- and every one is about a lap READING the wrong shared file. This is the same room's other wall: a lap WRITING into it and walking away.
obs I DELETED NOTHING, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. One pen was made the minute I looked, so some are live; and 32G of removal on a shared pier is Keaton's word rather than a passing lane's. The repair is copal's lane and is the trap every tools/fixtures/ control already carries.
obs THE COUNT SUGGESTS THIS IS NOT THE ONLY LEAKER. 41,063 entries in /tmp against 1,312 from this witness leaves a large remainder nobody has read. Counting them is its own lap and I did not open it.
